A||||7||KevinP||Thermostat Q's||||2||
Z||000000||KevinP||11-17-2000||08:03 PM||||while trying to track down my overheating problem, I pulled the thermostat and forgot to put it back in.  I know that it shouldn't cause any harm, but just what is the point of a thermostat?  My understanding is that it opens flow of coolant to the engine block when the engine reaches a certain temperature.  And don't cooler engines run more efficiently anyways?  Why not just have coolant always flowing through the engine?<P>Kevin<BR>'84 bumping 800 watts ||||reg||2
Z||000001||terryk||11-17-2000||08:11 PM||||It does a couple of things:<P>1. It forces the engine to warm up faster.<BR>2. It forces the coolant temp high enough to burn off moisture in the oil (bad juju).<BR>3. It stops large thermal cycling (large temp swings).<BR>4. It raises the operating temperture so the engine efficiency is better.<P>Without it, it could over heat since the coolant can pass through the radiator too quickly. My old 70 Firebird would do that.<P>Put it back in.<P>TK<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 11-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||11-17-2000||08:16 PM||||Here's one reason:<BR>The car would never reach proper operating temperature causing premature bearing wear and failure of most moving parts. (see below)<P>OH, here's another:<BR>The coolant would not efficently preform heat transfer because of too fast a flow. Result? Overheating.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hank is Here||11-17-2000||08:45 PM||||I know this from working on older vehicles but not having a thermostat in place can actually cause overheating.  The water can gain so much velocity that it can go through the radiator without cooling.  I seriously doubt that this would happenin a Fiero, especially this time of year but it can happen.<p>[This message has been edited by Hank is Here (edited 11-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||11-17-2000||10:46 PM||||i've been having the same problem for a few months now. steadily geeting worse. i almost wish it were leaking. i could look for it and fix it but every things full and i can't see a problem looks wise. both fill points full (some spills out from either when opened). guess i have to start swapping out stuff and hope i get it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DavidHenshaw||11-17-2000||11:51 PM||||Philphine, having coolant fluid full up isn't the whole story on overheating. Other possibilities include:<P>1. Improper colant mixture, should be about 50-50 water to antifreeze.<P>2. Is the water pump working or if the pumps working is there a clog somewhere in the system.<P>3. Is your thermostat stuck open or stuck closed. Either one is bad.<P>4. Is the radiator fouled with leaves or something else so air can't pass through.<P>5. Is the fan operating properly.<P>Hope this helps.<P>Dave<P>86 Gt||||reg||1
Z||000006||rnoswal||11-18-2000||12:08 AM||||Most cars I have owned and have run without a thermostat have run very cool. Helps a lot in the south. The main reason for a thermostat in a computer car is for emissions. If the engine never reaches the temp that the computer reads as optimum, then if low, it will always be running rich and poor fuel mileage and black exhaust. O2 sensors will cause some of the same problems if they are bad, rich running. So keep one in but you can put a lower temp thermostat in. Of course that is just my opinion and I could be wrong!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||11-18-2000||12:48 PM||||You definatly must use a thermostat for the ECM to work right. Preferably a lower temp fan switch as well.<P>There's a cave article that may help you decide which thermostat to use. the link is at the bottom of the page. presently it's in cooling 2, but since the index is getting some work done on it I'm probly going to change it's name. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-18-2000).]||||reg||1
